fez-mesh-controller for Debian
===============================

Installing this package creates a dedicated system user
(`fez-mesh-controller`) and a `fez-mesh-controller-daemon.service` systemd
unit, but does **not** start the daemon: it has no `config.toml` yet, and
starting it before one exists would just fail. Finish the setup manually:

1. Create the daemon's configuration, as the service user:

       sudo -u fez-mesh-controller fez-mesh-controller setup

   This writes `/var/lib/fez-mesh-controller/.config/fez-mesh-controller/config.toml`.

2. The packaged daemon listens on a fixed socket path, `RuntimeDirectory=`
   in the systemd unit, rather than the per-user default the CLI normally
   assumes (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, which an interactive admin session and a
   system service resolve completely differently):

       /run/fez-mesh-controller/fez-mesh-controller.sock

   When running the setup wizard above, or editing config.toml by hand,
   set `daemon.socket_path` to that path. Anyone using the CLI as
   themselves (not as the `fez-mesh-controller` user) needs their own
   config -- or the `--config` flag -- pointing at the same socket path to
   reach the packaged daemon.

3. Start the service:

       sudo systemctl start fez-mesh-controller-daemon
       journalctl -u fez-mesh-controller-daemon -f

It's already enabled (`systemctl enable`, done by this package's postinst),
so it starts automatically on every subsequent boot once step 1-2 are done.

A reference copy of every configuration parameter, with comments, is
installed alongside this file as config.example.toml.
